00fa0b9b (2350 1793615197404 "guenchi" (("4gm4c2jr" . 3240) ("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . section) (title . "landing f416") (src . "import-code --symbols: one cut supplier, moving ends, the human form\n\nThe symbols import split a file at an editor's ranges as it read them:\nevery start and every end a cut. Three consequences showed on a first\nimport of a real tree. A run whose every file was refused printed\nnothing and answered rc 0, because the human form of import-code kept\nonly incomplete after its items and dropped skipped, symbols-ignored,\nsymbols-imported-whole and symbols-refused. A cut at a symbol's start\nleft the comment run above it (a JSDoc) as an untitled block of its\nown, while split-suggest --symbols attaches that run to the definition\nbelow it: two answers for one question. And an editor's range that\nends inside a statement (const { a } = require('x') as { ... }, which\nan editor ends after the name) was put to the scanner as a cut, refused\nsymbols-not-top-level, and the whole file stayed out; the refusal did\nnot say which symbol.\n\nrender.sc: the four clauses join kept-after-items, so they are printed\nafter the items, one line each as the wire writes them, by import-code,\nimport --datum (its skipped clause) and uses (its skipped counts); the\nwidening reaches only answers of the items shape, a context answer\nprints through its own renderer as before.\n\ncode-suggest.sc: suggest-boundaries' strict answer gains a third\nelement, the start of every line the scan judged top level, not a\ncontinuation, holding code and not beginning a comment (in a fence\nprofile every non-blank line outside a fence); the judgment is written\nonce, in the scanner. The starts are kept in a table, not searched by\nmemv on every line. import-symbol-cuts takes its start cuts from the\nscanner's boundaries, as split-suggest does for the same symbols, so a\ncomment run above a definition goes with it and a cut at the protected\nprefix's end is dropped. An end is the import's own and is never\nrefused: it moves forward to the first code line at or after it and\ncuts there only when that line comes before the next block's start or\nthe file's end, so blank lines and a comment no definition takes go\nwith the block above, and a gap block holds code. The symbols, their\nends, the code lines and the cuts ascend together in one pass: in\nprocess, for JSDoc'd functions of five lines, import-symbol-cuts took\n21, 355 and 1399 ms at 1000, 4000 and 8000 symbols on the base, and\ntakes 7, 29 and 54 ms. A start\nthe scanner does not see at the top level is refused as before, with\n(symbol \"<name>\") added to the refusal. A block's title is the editor's\nname of the first symbol starting within it, where the derivation\nanswers nothing.\n\ncode-project.sc, rpc.sc and the README say so; the README's uses\nsection says the skipped counts print in the human form too.\n\ntest/code-symbols-import.sc is new, 21 rows: the human form of a run\nwhose every file was refused (one line, rc 0) and of a partial run (the\nitems, then the four clauses in the wire's order, byte for byte); the\ncuts of import-code --symbols equal to split-suggest --symbols' on a\nJSDoc'd TypeScript file, the blocks running together to the file, no\ncomment-only block, the const's editor title stored; the require shape\nimported as one statement with nothing refused; an end whose move\nreaches the next start making no gap block, one stopping at code\ncutting there, an end inside a multi-line template literal moving past\nit; a start inside a body refused at its byte with the symbol named and\nnothing written; import --datum's and uses' human forms ending with\ntheir skipped clause; and the cost of N and 4N JSDoc'd functions, the\n4N median at most six times the N (base 81 against 1426 ms). On the\nbase 13 rows are red. Five of six mutants red their rows (the clauses\nprinted only without items, the cuts taken from the starts as given, an\nend not moved, the refusal without its symbol, memv back in the strict\nscan); the sixth, the ends searched from the head, stays under the cost\nrow's bound at this size (ratio 5.5 against 6). The rows take their\nexpected values through with-expected, as the runner requires.\n\ntest/import-symbols.sc: 29 of 79 rows moved to the new rules (a comment\nrun joins its definition; an end is a cut only where code begins; a\nrefusal names the symbol; rows that asked the scanner's judgment of a\nline through an end now put a start there). test/code-suggest1.sc:\nCS-5 and CS-8 pin the third element. test/ecmascript-scan.sc: A8\ncompares cuts and warnings, the third element grows with the file.\ntest/change-stream-guard-expect.sexp lists describe: import-code's\ndescription changed. test/verb-registry-base.sexp: import-code's\ndescribe entry is the tree's, the other 48 unchanged. rows-baseline:\ncode-symbols-import added, import-symbols and ecmascript-scan hashes\nmoved, counts unchanged. Reviewed in two rounds; the second after the\nwhole suite found the verb-registry entry and the new fixture's\nunguarded expected values, both in tests.\n\nWhole suite on an isolated copy: 243 fixtures, not green only\nf0-ondemand and startup-ratio, the two known reds; the start-up\nalternation 0.9988 from source and 0.9898 compiled (median of three p10\nratios).\n") (parent . root) (ord . 603))))
